(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608273000-a-declared-interval-nothing-reads-back")
  (:adr/title "A declared interval that nothing reads back")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-27")
  (:adr/repo "cloud-itonami/otent")
  (:adr/body "# The interval was a control, and the schedule was defeating it

`otent` declares a `:min-interval-ms` per feed. That field has already been
caught being decorative once in this repository (2026-08-26: it sat in the
registry and nothing read it). On 2026-08-27 a coverage question was asked
by hand, and the answer found the second-order version of the same defect:
the field was being read, honoured to the millisecond, and **defeated one
layer up**.

## What was measured

Over 173 cycles / 23.4 hours of the timer era, from the tick ledger:

| | declared | measured (median) |
|---|---|---|
| cycle (plist `StartInterval`) | 300 s | 447 s |
| usgs | 300 s | 448 s |
| opensky | 600 s | 903 s |
| celestrak | 21,600 s | 22,032 s |

The mechanism: `bin/scheduled.cljs` ran `retain` inline after every tick,
retention takes ~3.4 minutes, and launchd will not start a job that is
still running. Every other 300-second fire was therefore dropped, and
`due?` -- answering honestly about a clock it was handed too rarely --
turned that into a 1.5x stretch on every feed's real poll rate.

**Nothing was red at any point.** The tick exited as expected, the receipt
ledger was complete, the tables were growing. The only symptom was a number
nobody was computing.

## Decisions

1. **`otent coverage`** (`src/otent/coverage.cljc`, pure) reads declared
   cadence back from the ledger and reports drift past a 1.25x tolerance.
   Exit 0 clean / 1 finding / 2 could-not-answer. A test asserts the
   tolerance is below 1.49x -- the drift it was written for -- because a
   tolerance chosen afterwards to make the current state pass is not a
   tolerance.
2. **`--window 3h`**, because the default median is over the whole ledger
   and a 30-hour median carries the old number for most of a day. An
   instrument built to measure a schedule that cannot show the schedule
   being repaired is useless at the one moment it matters. The window is
   printed in the header: *what is the cadence now* and *which window makes
   this look fine* are different questions, and the reader must be able to
   see which was asked.
3. **Retention leaves the tick's critical path**, running hourly against
   horizons whose shortest is a day. A skipped run logs `retain NOT-DUE`,
   never `retain exit 0`.
4. **The cycle times itself** and warns when it outlasts the timer's
   period, naming the consequence.
5. **Every feed declares `:scope`** -- what its request excludes and what
   could be asked for instead -- checked by a test. See below.

## The generalisable rule

**A declared interval is a claim about the world, and something has to read
it back.** `due?` checks whether the code MAY poll now; nothing was
checking how often it DID. Those are different questions, and the gap
between them is invisible to every green check.

The same shape appeared twice more in the same repository, both closed here:

- **An exemption whose clearing condition is prose will outlive its
  reason.** `firms` was exempt from being unmeasured because
  `$FIRMS_MAP_KEY` was not set, `:clears-when` read *the key is entered on
  this machine*, and so it sat there being true. The key is free; nobody
  had asked. It is now `the Keychain has firms.nasa/MAP_KEY`, which the
  cycle evaluates on every run -- a feed whose key it finds is no longer
  exempt. First commit: 27,833 global fire detections, a whole kind moved
  from never-observed to measured.
- **A scope decision can hide in a URL and stop looking like a decision.**
  The quake feed was `2.5_day.geojson` from day one. Every earthquake below
  M2.5 was not missing from the table, it was outside what was ever asked
  for -- and a narrowed request and a quiet world produce identical rows,
  so no table, receipt, governor or coverage report could tell them apart.
  Now `all_day.geojson`: 241 events against 29, smallest M-0.4, same
  parser.

## Closing the second gap rather than leaving it named

The first version of this ADR said cadence was now read back but the shape
of what a feed is asked for was not, and left that open. It is closed:
every registry entry declares `:scope` in prose -- what the request
excludes, and what could be asked for instead -- and a test refuses an
entry without one.

**This verifies nothing about whether a scope is right.** Nothing can, from
here. It makes it impossible to narrow coverage without writing down that
you did. `firms` is the case working as intended: it asks for VIIRS
NOAA-20 and names `VIIRS_SNPP_NRT`, `VIIRS_NOAA21_NRT` and `MODIS_NRT` as
available under the same key and not being asked for -- roughly a doubling
of detections, sitting in the registry as an arguable line rather than in a
path segment as a fact.

## Coverage as of 2026-08-27

| kind | rows | state |
|---|---|---|
| quake | 204 | live, all magnitudes |
| satellite | 21,085 | live, `GROUP=active`; 799 deep-space cannot be propagated |
| aircraft | 882,848 | live, bounded by OpenSky's receiver network |
| fire | 27,833 | live from 2026-08-27 |
| vessel | -- | **UNMEASURED**: AISStream is a WebSocket subscription and no resident collector exists. Structural, not a missing key. |

## Also measured, not fixed

The `tenkyu` -> `otent` rename copied the basemap and building objects to
the new prefix and never removed the old ones: **1,470 objects / 27.2 MB**
duplicated under `tenkyu/`, byte-identical apart from a `_probe.txt`. The
published manifests still named the retired prefix and were repointed; the
duplicate objects were left in place, because deleting 1,470 objects is the
owner's call and nothing reads them.

## What the same day found underneath it

Three more of the same shape, each closed:

**Nothing had a timeout.** The first cycle after the retention change sat
for eighteen minutes at 0% CPU, holding a dead OpenSky socket in
`CLOSE_WAIT` and then two established Cloudflare connections. Node's
`fetch` has no default timeout and nothing here set one. That is not a slow
poll: launchd will not start a job while the previous one is running, so
one stalled remote stops ingest entirely, and a cycle that never finishes
writes no receipt -- so the ledger shows nothing, which is what a quiet
period looks like. `otent.deadline` now bounds every call (60 s reads,
180 s writes, including the kotobase publish through `make-client`'s
`:fetch-fn`), and a call that runs out is UNMEASURED with `[timeout]`
rather than `[unreachable]`: one says the feed answered nothing, the other
says we stopped waiting and do not know what it would have said.

**The suite was green over a file that did not parse.** A one-paren edit
left `bin/otent.cljs` unparseable and `npm test` reported 70 tests, 740
assertions, 0 failures. That file is required by nothing -- it is the entry
point, it ends in `(-main)`, and requiring it from a test would run a tick
against the live feeds. So the one file that touches the network, holds the
commit logic and is what launchd executes was the one file no test could
see. Each CLI is now run bare (usage line, exit 2, no socket, but a real
load). `bin/scheduled.cljs` is still uncovered, and its docstring says so
rather than letting the floor look complete.

**The warning fired on the incident it was written for.** The new
cycle-duration warning printed `cycle took 994 s ... launchd will skip
fires` on that very stall -- in production, not in a test.

## Open: the satellite table is ingested and not servable

`GET /api/objects/satellite` has returned `503 error code: 1102` -- Worker
CPU exceeded -- on every attempt, ~45-50 s in, and raising `limits.cpu_ms`
to 120000 did not change it (the config was reverted rather than left in
place claiming to do something it does not).

Measured: `otent_satellite` is 21,085 rows in **5** Parquet files, 1.84 MB.
`otent_fire` returns 27,833 rows from **one** 0.93 MB file without
trouble. So the cost is bytes decoded, not rows returned, and the table has
been unservable since the catalogue was widened from `GROUP=stations` (21
objects) to `GROUP=active` on 2026-08-26 -- **ingest coverage went up and
read coverage silently went to zero**, with a 503 as the only sign. It will
worsen: the read window is seven days and each poll adds ~4,500 rows.

Narrowing the window does not fix it. Element sets arrive across polls
(21 + 7,493 + 4,684 + 4,412 + 4,475 to reach 21,085), so a short window
would hold a fraction of the catalogue rather than a stale copy of it.

The fix is a materialised current-catalogue object -- latest element set
per object, written by the ingest side where CPU is free, served by the
Worker without opening Parquet at all. **It is not done here**, because the
obvious quick version reimplements `app-otent`'s fold in a second language
in a second repository, and a second implementation of a fold drifts from
the first silently. Doing it properly means one fold both sides call, and
that is a design decision rather than an edit.

## Resolved 2026-08-28: one cause under three bug reports

The three symptoms this ADR reported as separate findings were one cause.
`cp/spawnSync` in the Iceberg writer blocked the Node event loop, and
`tick` runs its feeds under `js/Promise.all`, so:

- `AbortSignal.timeout` is a wall clock and kept counting while the process
  sat frozen inside a sibling's `python3`, producing `did not answer within
  60s` against a server that answered in 2.3 s from the shell;
- the per-feed elapsed time included being frozen, so it named the feed that
  finished last rather than the one that cost most;
- the cycle outran its own 300 s period, launchd declined to start an
  overlapping job, and every feed's effective interval stretched.

`run-writer!` now spawns and resolves. Measured on the verifying run: a
NOTHING-NEW feed finished in **1 s while a sibling was 26 s into a commit**,
and three feeds totalling 82 s of per-feed work completed in **49.6 s of
wall clock**. On the live timer, cycles went 282 s -> 111 s -> 30 s -> 5 s.

**The accident had been holding an invariant.** `spawnSync` also serialised
every commit in the process. `otent.lock` restores that deliberately, per
table, so two feeds sharing a kind cannot each read a `before` count
containing the other's rows -- and different tables still overlap, which is
the point. A rejected write does not wedge the chain; without that, one
refusal turns a table dead and reads from outside as the feed going quiet.

**The refactor shipped a bug the suite could not see.** `(.then p :rows)` is
correct-looking and wrong: a ClojureScript keyword is a function to Clojure
and an opaque object to `Promise.prototype.then`, so it returned the whole
status map, the delta computed `NaN`, and two commits that had ACTUALLY
LANDED were reported `count-mismatch`. Nothing in the suite exercises
`commit!` against a live catalog, so only a real tick found it. The failure
ran in the safe direction and that was luck, not design.

**A second writer now exists on purpose**, so `commit!` records which check
answered: an exact delta is `:delta`; growth by more than this commit wrote
means another process appended between the counts and is `:presence-only`
with a note; growth by less is still a refusal. Refusing the middle case
would report two successful commits as a fault, and accepting it silently
would let a weaker check wear the strong one's name.

## What the instrument found after it existed

`otent coverage` was written to read a declared interval back. Over the two
days after it landed it also found, each time by measurement rather than
inspection:

- a prefilter matched `\\\"schema\\\": \\\"` with a space where the export emits
  none -- it would have rejected every line in production while passing a
  fixture written by `json.dumps`, and an empty index looks exactly like a
  sanctions list with nobody on it;
- a deadline blaming the remote for this process's own blocking;
- a dark-feed refusal that stopped retention for 51 minutes because a
  DIFFERENT feed could not be read;
- a `node_modules` symlink committed to main, because `.gitignore`'s
  `node_modules/` matches a directory and not a symlink.

The recurring shape is the one this ADR opened with: **something that could
not be measured returning the same value as something measured and found
fine.** It appeared six more times in two days, in six different places.
")))
